In 'The Ballad of Liberation from the Khandhas', the teaching opens with this observation: *"I pay homage to the one Well-gone,
the Foremost Teacher, the Sakyan Sage,
the Rightly Self-Awakened One;
& to the nine transcendent Dhammas;
& to the Noble Saṅgha."* The central invitation is to turn one's investigation inward — to verify the Dhamma not as an external philosophy, but as directly observable reality in one's own body, speech, and mind. As the text emphasizes, the Dhamma is *akāliko* (timeless) and *opanayiko* (leading inward), realizable (*paccattaṁ*) by each practitioner through patient, honest awareness.

In the section 'The Ballad of Liberation', the text points out: *"I pay homage to the one Well-gone,
the Foremost Teacher, the Sakyan Sage,
the Rightly Self-Awakened One;
& to the nine transcendent Dhammas;
& to the Noble Saṅgha...."* This part of the teaching instructs the meditator to look closely at the underlying movements of the mind rather than getting lost in superficial reactions. By maintaining steady, unattached mindfulness, we allow mental formations (*saṅkhāras*) to arise, reveal their impermanent nature, and cease without grasping or resistance. This direct seeing is the true doorway to peace.

In 'The Ballad of Liberation from the Khandhas', Dhamma is discussed in this context: *"[Section: The Ballad of Liberation]
I pay homage to the one Well-gone,
the Foremost Teacher, the Sakyan Sage,
the Rightly Self-Awakened One;
& to the nine transcendent Dhammas;
& to the Noble Saṅgha."* Here, Dhamma (the Dhamma — the truth, the teaching, the nature of reality) is treated not as a theoretical doctrine, but as a living faculty to be actively cultivated and observed. When we bring genuine mindfulness to our direct experience, the quality of Dhamma helps dismantle habitual delusion and stabilizes the mind in clear, unentangled awareness.
